    {
      "question": "e-7902",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "f3aaa9265d925f03201cd4ec5af11ad55dbda1bc0f10aa1c0171beb875caf1ab",
      "new_sha256": "3d9749bcf69104df78de067d56f178871464297afa15792bd079fabd07a48db9",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-7902",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{3}+3x & <0. \\\\ 3x(2{x}^{2}+1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2x^2 + 1$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x(2{x}^{2}+1)}{2{x}^{2}+1} & <\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ 3x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/example-7902.json",
          "file_sha256": "033aa455195115a6fc29dbde28716cc957fbb843974c49b56635ba8f1833d037"
        }
