    {
      "question": "e-7903",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "63b8d935e8dd68016f269366d9044f67448acd0cebb9130cffaaef1768b99fe2",
      "new_sha256": "ca0db27604ea659afad5bae99ab07d2321e652c08d3dab2f635f1f126aea06e5",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-7903",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Note that the factor $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad -11 \\leq 2\\sin x - 9 \\leq -7.\n$$\n\nBecause $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-9)(2x) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-9)(2x)}{2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-9} & \\ge \\frac{0}{2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-9} \\\\ 2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/example-7903.json",
          "file_sha256": "fc920db1fc4edad957c45a7cb6d7646b4119e7d131d1919fb6da7f082db0fc92"
        }
