    {
      "question": "e-7904",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "4639435b3709e0e1cb4b19288692989a6dfa16bc7b22572d98b17550ead0f101",
      "new_sha256": "e1228667b0f29f783bb31af19ad64e01ccdbe6195e4e56aee04e2c29d728fdaa",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-7904",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Since $x\\lt 0,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <0 \\\\ -x & >0 \\\\ -3x & >0 \\\\ 1-3x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1-3x$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x\\lt 0).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $1-3x,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+5}{1-3x} & <2 \\\\ (1-3x)\\cdot \\frac{x+5}{1-3x} & <(1-3x)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x+5 & <2-6x \\\\ 7x & <-3 \\\\ x & <-\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x \\lt 0$ and $x \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981762/example-7904.json",
          "file_sha256": "e02854fdee15071489a23d7594b26a96ded565045b062922650db6a87b4cc2f3"
        }
